# ANTI OVERENGINEERING

Patterns are allowed when they make code simpler to read or use:

- Factories (static `create(...)` for simple objects, a factory class only for complex construction)
- Builders (`@Builder(toBuilder = true)` on DTOs)
- Provider / Strategy (only where the spec names multiple implementations: cache, storage, messaging, mail, configuration)
- Wrappers (only the standard ones: `ApiResponse`, `ApiError`, `BaseResult`)

Strictly avoid overengineering:

- No abstraction with a single imaginary future use. One real use → no interface beyond the required port.
- No generic frameworks, reflection tricks, or meta-programming when plain code works.
- No extra layers, decorators, or specifications unless requested.
- No configuration options nobody asked for.
- A junior developer must understand any class in under a minute. If not, simplify.

Prefer the simplest valid implementation.
